export default {"cls": "mm","label": "Math Modeling","items": [{"id": "MM.1-01","std": "MM.1","q": "In the Mathematical Modeling Cycle, what is the first step when solving a real-world problem such as reducing school cafeteria food waste?","a": "Identify the problem and define the variables","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Identify the problem and define the variables","Verify the model against new data","Report the final results","Perform a regression analysis"],"steps": ["Think about what you must do before collecting data or doing math.","You first need to know what the problem is and what can change.","Match that starting step to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.1-02","std": "MM.1","q": "A student models the monthly cost of running a lawn-mowing business. Which of the following is an assumption rather than a measured fact?","a": "Gas prices will remain at §3.50 per gallon all summer","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Each customer pays §35 per lawn","Gas prices will remain at §3.50 per gallon all summer","The student mowed 12 lawns last week","The lawn mower cost §420 at the store"],"steps": ["An assumption is a guess or condition you treat as true.","Measured facts come from prices paid or counts already made.","Find the choice about a future guess and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.1-03","std": "MM.1","q": "In the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle, after formulating a statistical question and collecting data, what is the next step?","a": "Analyze the data","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Analyze the data","Formulate a new question","Publish the conclusion","Discard outliers"],"steps": ["List the steps of the statistical cycle in order.","After you ask a question and gather data, you study the data.","Pick the step that means studying data and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-04","std": "MM.1","q": "A school club plans a car wash fundraiser. Supplies cost §60, and they charge §10 per car. Their initial model for profit is P = 10c − 60. If they wash 25 cars, what is the predicted profit?","a": "§190","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§150","§310","§250","§190"],"steps": ["Plug the number of cars into the profit equation.","Replace c with 25 to get P = 10 × 25 − 60.","Multiply first, then subtract, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-05","std": "MM.1","q": "A student models daily water usage in a household as W = 50p + 30, where p is the number of people and W is gallons per day. A 4-person household actually uses 280 gallons per day. What is the difference between the actual usage and the model's prediction?","a": "50 gallons","lvl": 2,"choices": ["230 gallons","30 gallons","50 gallons","80 gallons"],"steps": ["Find the model's prediction for 4 people, then compare to actual.","Compute W = 50 × 4 + 30, then subtract that from 280.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-06","std": "MM.1","q": "A city models park attendance with A = 120 + 15t, where t is temperature in °F above 60°F. When the temperature is 80°F, attendance is predicted to be 420. If rain reduces attendance by 40%, what is the revised prediction?","a": "252 people","lvl": 2,"choices": ["168 people","288 people","380 people","252 people"],"steps": ["A 40% drop leaves 60% of the predicted attendance.","Set up 420 × 0.60, or find 40% of 420 and subtract it.","Finish the calculation and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-07","std": "MM.1","q": "A student's model for coffee shop revenue is R = 4.50c, where c is cups sold. After testing the model for a week, actual revenue is consistently 15% higher than predicted because customers also buy pastries. How should the student revise the model in the next iteration of the modeling cycle?","a": "Add a variable or average pastry revenue per customer to the equation","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Add a variable or average pastry revenue per customer to the equation","Stop the modeling cycle because the error is under 20%","Ignore pastry sales because they were not in the original plan","Change the price of coffee in the model to §4.50"],"steps": ["Ask why actual revenue is always higher than the model predicts.","A good model update includes the missing source of revenue.","Choose the revision that adds pastry sales and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-08","std": "MM.1","q": "Two models predict monthly electric bills (y) from square footage (x): Model A is y = 0.12x + 25 and Model B is y = 0.10x + 65. For a 1,500 sq ft home with an actual bill of §210, which model is more accurate and by how much?","a": "Both models have the exact same error","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Both models have the exact same error","Model B is more accurate by §5","Model A is more accurate by §5","Model A is more accurate by §10"],"steps": ["Plug x = 1,500 into both equations to get each predicted bill.","Find each model's error by taking the gap from §210.","Compare the two errors, find the difference, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.1-09","std": "MM.1","q": "A school asks if roof solar panels pay off over 15 years. Which plan follows the Mathematical Modeling Cycle?","a": "Define cost and energy variables, assume a fixed rate, build, test, refine","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Install the panels first, then see if they pay off","Define cost and energy variables, assume a fixed rate, build, test, refine","Pick the cheapest panels without building a model","Look up one neighbor's electric bill and decide"],"steps": ["List four stages: define, assume, build a model, and test or refine.","Pick two key cost or energy variables and one simple assumption.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.2-01","std": "MM.2","q": "Maya earns §2,400 per month after taxes. Her monthly expenses total §1,950. How much money can she save each month?","a": "§450","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§550","§350","§4,350","§450"],"steps": ["Savings equal income minus expenses.","Set up the subtraction: §2,400 − §1,950.","Subtract carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-02","std": "MM.2","q": "Devon wants an emergency fund equal to 3 months of essential expenses. His essential expenses are §1,600 per month. What is his target emergency fund?","a": "§4,800","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§6,400","§5,400","§4,800","§3,200"],"steps": ["Multiply one month of expenses by the number of months.","Set up the product: §1,600 × 3.","Find the total and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-03","std": "MM.2","q": "A part-time job pays §16 per hour. If Jordan works 20 hours per week and 15% is withheld for taxes, what is Jordan's weekly take-home pay?","a": "§272","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§320","§272","§48","§288"],"steps": ["First find gross pay, then keep the 85% left after taxes.","Multiply §16 × 20, then multiply that gross pay by 0.85.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-04","std": "MM.2","q": "Elena has §600 saved and plans to save §225 each month for a §2,400 used car. How many months will it take her to reach her goal?","a": "8 months","lvl": 2,"choices": ["11 months","6 months","8 months","10 months"],"steps": ["Find how much more money Elena still needs to save.","Subtract §2,400 − §600, then divide that amount by §225.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-05","std": "MM.2","q": "Three roommates split §1,800 in rent and §240 in utilities equally. If one roommate also pays §60 for internet alone, what is that roommate's total monthly housing cost?","a": "§740","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§680","§740","§700","§660"],"steps": ["Add the shared bills first, split 3 ways, then add internet.","Set up (§1,800 + §240) ÷ 3 + §60.","Finish the operations in order and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-06","std": "MM.2","q": "A freelance designer charges §50 per hour, sets aside 25% of gross income for taxes, and pays §150 per month for software. If she works 40 hours in a month, what is her net income?","a": "§1,350","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,350","§1,500","§1,850","§1,387.50"],"steps": ["Find gross pay, take out 25% for taxes, then subtract software.","Compute §50 × 40 × 0.75, then subtract §150.","Finish simplifying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-07","std": "MM.2","q": "Job A pays §3,200/month after taxes with a §280/month commute cost. Job B pays §3,050/month after taxes and is remote (no commute cost), but requires §60/month in upgraded internet. Which job yields more net monthly income and by how much?","a": "Job B by §70 per month","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Job B by §70 per month","Job B by §130 per month","Job A by §150 per month","Job A by §70 per month"],"steps": ["Find net monthly income for each job by subtracting its extra cost.","Compare (§3,200 − §280) with (§3,050 − §60).","Find the difference between the two nets and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-08","std": "MM.2","q": "Carlos budgets §2,800/month: 40% for rent, 20% for food, 25% for other expenses, and 15% for savings. Next year his rent increases by 10% while his income stays the same. If he keeps food and other expenses unchanged, how much will he have left for monthly savings?","a": "§308","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§140","§420","§378","§308"],"steps": ["Find the original rent and savings, or find how much rent rises.","Find 10% of the 40% rent share and subtract that increase from savings.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.2-09","std": "MM.2","q": "A graduate earns §3,500 a month after taxes and wants to save §6,000 in 12 months while paying §1,200 rent, §350 student loans and §450 food. Is the goal reachable?","a": "Yes: needs §500 a month and leaves §1,000 for other costs","lvl": 3,"choices": ["No: needs §600 a month, but only §500 is left","Yes: needs §500 a month and leaves §2,000 for other costs","No: the fixed costs are more than the income","Yes: needs §500 a month and leaves §1,000 for other costs"],"steps": ["Find the monthly savings needed by dividing §6,000 by 12.","Subtract all listed bills and monthly savings from §3,500.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.3-01","std": "MM.3","q": "An account starts with §2,000 and earns 5% simple interest per year. What is the balance after 3 years?","a": "§2,300","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§2,300","§2,315.25","§2,150","§2,100"],"steps": ["Simple interest uses I = P × r × t, then add I to P.","Find interest with §2,000 × 0.05 × 3, then add §2,000.","Add carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-02","std": "MM.3","q": "A delivery van is purchased for §24,000 and depreciates by §3,000 per year using straight-line depreciation. What is its value after 4 years?","a": "§12,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§18,000","§9,000","§12,000","§15,000"],"steps": ["Straight-line depreciation subtracts the same amount each year.","Set up §24,000 − (§3,000 × 4).","Multiply first, then subtract, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-03","std": "MM.3","q": "A bond's value each year forms the arithmetic sequence §500, §540, §580, §620, ... What is the annual simple interest earned?","a": "§40","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§80","§540","§40","§20"],"steps": ["In an arithmetic sequence, find the constant change between terms.","Subtract two neighboring terms, like §540 − §500.","Verify the same jump works for all terms and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-04","std": "MM.3","q": "A commercial printer costs §1,800 and has a salvage value of §200 after 8 years of straight-line depreciation. What is its value after 5 years?","a": "§800","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,000","§600","§675","§800"],"steps": ["First find the yearly depreciation, then subtract 5 years of it.","Find (§1,800 − §200) ÷ 8 per year, then subtract 5 times that from §1,800.","Finish the calculation and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-05","std": "MM.3","q": "A small business borrows §4,500 at 6% annual simple interest for 4 years. What is the total amount to be repaid at the end of 4 years?","a": "§5,580","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§5,681.15","§5,580","§1,080","§4,770"],"steps": ["Find total simple interest over 4 years, then add the loan amount.","Set up §4,500 + (§4,500 × 0.06 × 4).","Finish computing the total and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-06","std": "MM.3","q": "A farm tractor purchased for §35,000 depreciates by §2,500 each year. After how many years will its book value be §15,000?","a": "8 years","lvl": 2,"choices": ["6 years","10 years","14 years","8 years"],"steps": ["Find the total value lost, then divide by the yearly drop.","Set up (§35,000 − §15,000) ÷ §2,500.","Divide to find the years and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-07","std": "MM.3","q": "Bond X invests §8,000 at 4.5% simple interest per year. Bond Y invests §6,000 at 6% simple interest per year. After 5 years, how do their total values compare?","a": "Bond X is worth §2,000 more than Bond Y","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Bond X is worth §1,800 more than Bond Y","Bond Y is worth more than Bond X","They have the exact same value","Bond X is worth §2,000 more than Bond Y"],"steps": ["Compute the ending value of each bond using simple interest.","Use A = P + P × r × t for both Bond X and Bond Y with t = 5.","Compare the two final values and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-08","std": "MM.3","q": "A bakery buys an oven for §12,000 and depreciates it using straight-line depreciation to a salvage value of §2,400 over 6 years. If the bakery sells the oven after 3 years for §6,500, how does the sale price compare to its book value?","a": "§700 less than book value","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§500 more than book value","§700 more than book value","§700 less than book value","§1,600 less than book value"],"steps": ["Find the yearly drop, then find the book value after 3 years.","Compute yearly loss from (§12,000 − §2,400) ÷ 6, then compare to §6,500.","Find if §6,500 is above or below book value and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.3-09","std": "MM.3","q": "A §10,000 loan earns 5% simple interest per year, and a §10,000 machine loses §1,250 per year. Which explicit formulas model them?","a": "Loan: A(n) = 10000 + 500n; machine: V(n) = 10000 − 1250n","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Loan: A(n) = 10000(1.05)^n; machine: V(n) = 10000(0.875)^n","Loan: A(n) = 500n; machine: V(n) = 1250n","Loan: A(n) = 10000 + 5n; machine: V(n) = 10000 − 1250n","Loan: A(n) = 10000 + 500n; machine: V(n) = 10000 − 1250n"],"steps": ["Connect constant dollar change per year to a common difference.","Use start + or − (yearly change) × n for each formula.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.4-01","std": "MM.4","q": "You deposit §1,000 in an account earning 10% interest compounded annually. What is the balance after 2 years?","a": "§1,210","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§1,200","§1,210","§1,220","§1,100"],"steps": ["Compound interest multiplies by (1 + r) each year.","Set up A = §1,000 × (1.10)².","Evaluate the power, multiply, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-02","std": "MM.4","q": "A laptop costs §1,000 and loses 20% of its value each year. What is its value after 2 years?","a": "§640","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§800","§400","§600","§640"],"steps": ["Losing 20% each year means keeping 80%, or 0.80, each year.","Set up V = §1,000 × (0.80)².","Square 0.80, multiply by 1,000, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-03","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "A savings account has an APR of 6% compounded once per year. What is its APY?","a": "6%","lvl": 1,"choices": ["12%","6%","0.5%","6.17%"],"steps": ["APY is the effective rate earned over one full year.","With compounding once per year, use (1 + 0.06)¹ − 1.","Convert back to a percent and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-04","std": "MM.4","q": "An investment of §5,000 earns 4% APR compounded quarterly. What is the balance after 3 years?","a": "§5,634.13","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§5,600.00","§5,624.32","§5,637.48","§5,634.13"],"steps": ["Use A = P(1 + {r/n})^{nt} with n = 4 quarters per year.","Plug in P = 5,000, r = 0.04, n = 4, and nt = 12.","Evaluate with a calculator, round to cents, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-05","std": "MM.4","q": "An account with §2,000 earns 5% APR compounded continuously. Using A = Pe^{rt}, what is the balance after 4 years?","a": "§2,442.81","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§2,460.00","§2,442.81","§2,431.01","§2,400.00"],"steps": ["Plug principal, rate as a decimal, and time into A = Pe^{rt}.","Set up A = 2,000e^{0.05 × 4}.","Multiply the exponent first, compute, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-06","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "A credit union offers 12% APR compounded monthly. What is the APY rounded to the nearest hundredth of a percent?","a": "12.68%","lvl": 2,"choices": ["13.00%","12.68%","12.36%","12.00%"],"steps": ["Use APY = (1 + {r/n})^{n} − 1 with n = 12 months.","Plug in r = 0.12 and n = 12 to get (1 + 0.01)^{12} − 1.","Turn the decimal into a percent, round, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-07","std": "MM.4","q": "A new SUV costs §30,000 and depreciates by 15% per year. What is its value after 5 years, rounded to the nearest cent?","a": "§13,311.16","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§11,314.49","§7,500.00","§13,311.16","§15,660.19"],"steps": ["A 15% yearly drop gives a decay factor of 1 − 0.15.","Set up V = 30,000 × (0.85)⁵.","Evaluate, round to the nearest cent, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-08","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "Bank A offers 6% APR compounded monthly. Bank B offers 5.9% APR compounded continuously. Which bank has the higher APY and by how much (rounded to the nearest hundredth of a percent)?","a": "Bank A by 0.09%","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Bank A by 0.09%","Bank A by 0.10%","Bank B by 0.09%","Both have the same APY"],"steps": ["Convert both banks to APY so you can compare them fairly.","Compute (1 + {0.06/12})^{12} − 1 for A and e^{0.059} − 1 for B.","Subtract the rounded percents and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.4-09","std": "MM.4","part": "a","q": "Why is APY greater than APR when interest compounds more than once a year?","a": "Interest earned early in the year also earns interest later","lvl": 3,"choices": ["APY includes bank fees that APR leaves out","Interest earned early in the year also earns interest later","APY is always double the APR","APR is calculated with a larger principal"],"steps": ["Identify which rate is nominal (APR) and which is effective (APY).","Match APR to r in Pe^{rt} and APY to r in P(1 + r)^{t}.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.5-01","std": "MM.5","q": "You invest §1,000 at 10% for 2 years. How much more does annual compound interest earn than simple interest?","a": "§10","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§0","§10","§20","§100"],"steps": ["Find the ending balance for both simple and compound interest.","Compare §1,000 + (§1,000 × 0.10 × 2) with §1,000 × (1.10)².","Subtract the two balances and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-02","std": "MM.5","q": "A §10,000 machine is modeled by straight-line depreciation (§2,000/year) and proportional depreciation (20%/year). After 2 years, how much higher is the proportional depreciation value?","a": "§400","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§0","§800","§400","§1,600"],"steps": ["Find the 2-year value under each depreciation model.","Compare §10,000 − 2(§2,000) with §10,000 × (0.80)².","Find the difference between them and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-03","std": "MM.5","q": "For the same principal and positive annual interest rate over 10 years, which type of interest produces a larger ending balance?","a": "Compound interest","lvl": 1,"choices": ["It depends on the principal","Compound interest","Simple interest","Both produce the same balance"],"steps": ["Think about how interest grows over multiple years.","Compound interest earns interest on past interest, not just principal.","Pick the faster-growing type and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-04","std": "MM.5","q": "Compare §4,000 invested at 5% simple interest versus 5% interest compounded annually for 3 years. How much more does the compound account have?","a": "§30.50","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§630.50","§10.00","§60.00","§30.50"],"steps": ["Compute the 3-year balance for simple and for compound interest.","Compare §4,000 + (§4,000 × 0.05 × 3) with §4,000 × (1.05)³.","Subtract the smaller from the larger and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-05","std": "MM.5","q": "A §20,000 truck is modeled by straight-line depreciation of §3,000/year and proportional depreciation of 15%/year. After 3 years, what is the difference between the two values?","a": "§1,282.50","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,282.50","§900.00","§1,450.00","§3,000.00"],"steps": ["Find both 3-year truck values, then find how far apart they are.","Compare §20,000 − 3(§3,000) with §20,000 × (0.85)³.","Subtract to find the gap and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-06","std": "MM.5","q": "A §15,000 generator loses §3,000/year under straight-line depreciation and 20%/year under proportional depreciation. When does each model reach a value of §0?","a": "Straight-line reaches §0 in 5 years; proportional never reaches §0","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Both reach §0 in 5 years","Proportional reaches §0 in 5 years; straight-line never reaches §0","Straight-line reaches §0 in 5 years; proportional never reaches §0","Neither model reaches §0"],"steps": ["Test when each model can actually equal §0.","Solve 15,000 − 3,000t = 0 and check if 15,000 × (0.80)^{t} can be 0.","Match both behaviors to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.5-07","std": "MM.5","q": "Account A invests §5,000 at 6% simple interest. Account B invests §5,000 at 5% interest compounded annually. After 10 years, which account has more money and by how much?","a": "Account B by §144.47","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Account B by §144.47","Account A by §500.00","Account B by §288.95","Account A by §144.47"],"steps": ["Find both 10-year balances using the right formula for each account.","Compare 5,000(1 + 0.06 × 10) against 5,000 × (1.05)^{10}.","Find which is larger, take the difference, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-08","std": "MM.5","q": "A §12,000 piece of equipment loses §2,000/year under straight-line depreciation and 20%/year under proportional depreciation. In which year does the proportional value first become greater than the straight-line value?","a": "Year 3","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Year 4","Year 2","Year 3","Year 1"],"steps": ["Make a year-by-year table for both models starting at Year 1.","Subtract §2,000 each year for one, and multiply by 0.80 for the other.","Find the first year proportional is higher and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.5-09","std": "MM.5","q": "Why does proportional depreciation fit vehicle resale better than straight-line depreciation?","a": "Cars lose the most value early and never fall to §0","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Cars gain value after the first year","Cars lose the same dollar amount every year","Cars lose the most value early and never fall to §0","Proportional depreciation is simpler for taxes"],"steps": ["Compare how each model changes dollar value from year to year.","Link equal yearly drops to taxes, and fast early drops to car resale.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.6-01","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has a beginning balance of §600. During the month, the holder makes a §150 payment, §100 in new purchases, and is charged §10 in interest. What is the ending balance?","a": "§560","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§660","§540","§560","§860"],"steps": ["Payments lower a credit card balance; purchases and interest raise it.","Set up §600 − §150 + §100 + §10.","Compute left to right and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-02","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has an APR of 18%. What is the monthly periodic interest rate?","a": "1.5%","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1.5%","0.15%","18%","1.8%"],"steps": ["APR is the yearly rate, and there are 12 months in a year.","Divide the APR by 12: 18% ÷ 12.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-03","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card carries a §1,000 unpaid balance at a 2% monthly interest rate. What is the finance charge for the month?","a": "§20","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§20","§2","§24","§200"],"steps": ["Multiply the unpaid balance by the monthly interest rate.","Convert 2% to 0.02 and set up §1,000 × 0.02.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-04","std": "MM.6","q": "In a credit card spreadsheet, beginning balance is §800, payment is §200, new purchases are §150, and a 1.5% monthly interest rate is charged on (beginning balance − payment). What is the ending balance?","a": "§759","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§764.25","§762","§759","§750"],"steps": ["Subtract the payment first, charge 1.5% on that, then add purchases.","Set up (§800 − §200) × 1.015 + §150.","Simplify step by step and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-05","std": "MM.6","q": "A credit card has a §1,200 balance at 24% APR (2% per month) with no new purchases. Each month, interest is added first and then a §200 payment is made. What is the balance after 2 months?","a": "§844.48","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§1,024.00","§844.48","§848.00","§800.00"],"steps": ["Track one month at a time: multiply by 1.02, then subtract §200.","Find Month 1's end balance, then repeat the same rule for Month 2.","Round to the nearest cent and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-06","std": "MM.6","q": "A cardholder with a §500 balance misses a payment and is charged a §35 late fee. Then 2% monthly interest is applied to the new total. What is the resulting balance?","a": "§545.70","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§535.00","§545.70","§510.00","§545.00"],"steps": ["Add the late fee first because interest applies to the new total.","Set up (§500 + §35) × 1.02.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-07","std": "MM.6","q": "In a spreadsheet, B2 is beginning balance, C2 is payment, D2 is new purchases, and E2 is late fee. Interest of 1.5% is charged on (B2 − C2). Which formula correctly computes the ending balance in F2?","a": "=(B2-C2) × 1.015+D2+E2","lvl": 3,"choices": ["=(B2+D2-C2) × 0.015+E2","=(B2-C2) × 1.015+D2+E2","=(B2-C2+D2+E2) × 1.015","=B2 × 1.015-C2+D2+E2"],"steps": ["Match each cell to its role and note which part earns 1.5% interest.","Multiply only (B2 − C2) by 1.015, then add D2 and E2 outside.","Match that exact structure to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.6-08","std": "MM.6","q": "A §2,000 credit card balance has a 24% APR (2% monthly). If the cardholder makes no new purchases and pays §40 each month, what happens to the balance over time?","a": "The balance stays at §2,000 forever because the payment only covers monthly interest","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The balance is paid off in 50 months","The balance drops by §40 every month","The balance grows by §40 every month","The balance stays at §2,000 forever because the payment only covers monthly interest"],"steps": ["Find one month's interest charge and compare it to the §40 payment.","Compute §2,000 × 0.02 to see how much of the payment lowers principal.","Decide what happens to the balance each month and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.6-09","std": "MM.6","q": "In a credit card payoff spreadsheet, why does paying §50 above the minimum cut total interest so much?","a": "The extra goes to principal, shrinking the balance that earns interest","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The bank waives interest on payments over the minimum","The extra goes to principal, shrinking the balance that earns interest","Extra payments only shorten the time, not the interest","It lowers the card's APR"],"steps": ["List the columns needed: Month, Start, Interest, Payment, and End.","Show how End = Start + Interest − Payment and carries to the next row.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.7-01","std": "MM.7","q": "An apartment rents for §1,200 per month plus §20 per month for renter's insurance. What is the total annual housing cost?","a": "§14,640","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§14,400","§14,420","§1,220","§14,640"],"steps": ["Add the two monthly costs, then multiply by 12 months.","Set up (§1,200 + §20) × 12.","Finish the math and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-02","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "A buyer purchases a §200,000 home with a 20% down payment. How much is the mortgage loan?","a": "§160,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§180,000","§240,000","§160,000","§40,000"],"steps": ["A 20% down payment leaves 80% of the price to borrow.","Set up §200,000 × 0.80, or subtract 20% from §200,000.","Finish computing the loan amount and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-03","std": "MM.7","part": "a","q": "What is the main advantage of a 30-year fixed-rate mortgage compared to an adjustable-rate mortgage (ARM)?","a": "The interest rate and monthly principal-and-interest payment stay the same for the entire loan","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The interest rate and monthly principal-and-interest payment stay the same for the entire loan","It pays off the home in half the time","It always has a lower initial interest rate","It requires no down payment"],"steps": ["Compare what 'fixed-rate' means versus 'adjustable-rate'.","A fixed rate never changes, so your main monthly payment stays steady.","Find the choice about predictable payments and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.7-04","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "For the same loan amount, a 30-year mortgage has a §1,500 monthly payment and a 15-year mortgage has a §2,100 monthly payment. How much less is paid in total over the life of the 15-year loan?","a": "§162,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§600","§216,000","§108,000","§162,000"],"steps": ["Find the total paid over all months for each mortgage.","Compare §1,500 × 360 months with §2,100 × 180 months.","Subtract the two totals and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-05","std": "MM.7","q": "A lease-to-purchase contract charges §1,800/month for 24 months, with §300/month credited toward the down payment, plus an upfront §5,000 option fee also credited to the down payment. What total amount is credited toward the down payment after 24 months?","a": "§12,200","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§7,200","§48,200","§43,200","§12,200"],"steps": ["Only count the amounts that go toward the down payment.","Set up (§300 × 24) + §5,000.","Multiply first, add the fee, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-06","std": "MM.7","part": "c","q": "Renting costs §1,400/month while owning a comparable home costs §1,900/month. If a renter invests the monthly savings, how much principal will they invest over 3 years (ignoring investment returns)?","a": "§18,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§18,000","§50,400","§15,000","§6,000"],"steps": ["Find the monthly savings, then multiply by the months in 3 years.","Set up (§1,900 − §1,400) × 36.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-07","std": "MM.7","part": "b","q": "For a §300,000 home, Option A uses 10% down and a 30-year payment of §1,700/month. Option B uses 20% down and a 30-year payment of §1,440/month. Including the down payment, how much less total cash does Option B cost over 30 years?","a": "§63,600","lvl": 3,"choices": ["§30,000","§63,600","§33,600","§93,600"],"steps": ["Find total cash for each option: down payment plus 360 payments.","Compute Option A and Option B totals using 10% and 20% of §300,000.","Subtract Option B from Option A and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-08","std": "MM.7","part": "c","q": "A buyer purchases a §250,000 home with §50,000 down, and the home appreciates 3% per year for 5 years. A renter invests that §50,000 at 7% compounded annually for 5 years. Which grows by more dollars over 5 years, the home's value or the renter's investment?","a": "The home's value grows by §19,690.93 more","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The home's value grows by §39,818.52 more","The renter's investment grows by §19,690.93 more","Both grow by the same dollar amount","The home's value grows by §19,690.93 more"],"steps": ["Find the dollar gain (ending minus starting) for both options.","Use 250,000 × (1.03)⁵ − 250,000 and 50,000 × (1.07)⁵ − 50,000.","Compare the two dollar gains, take their gap, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.7-09","std": "MM.7","part": "a","q": "Compared with an FHA loan at 3.5% down, what is the main advantage and disadvantage of a conventional loan with 20% down?","a": "Advantage: no PMI. Disadvantage: much more cash needed up front","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Advantage: easier credit rules. Disadvantage: higher interest","Advantage: less cash up front. Disadvantage: mortgage insurance","Advantage: no interest. Disadvantage: shorter loan term","Advantage: no PMI. Disadvantage: much more cash needed up front"],"steps": ["Compare upfront cash needed, monthly payments, and mortgage insurance.","List one pro and one con for conventional, then one pro and con for FHA.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.8-01","std": "MM.8","q": "A car lease costs §300 per month for 36 months plus §2,000 due at signing. What is the total cost of the lease?","a": "§12,800","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§10,800","§12,800","§11,000","§14,800"],"steps": ["Add all monthly lease payments to the upfront signing cost.","Set up (§300 × 36) + §2,000.","Multiply first, then add, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-02","std": "MM.8","q": "A used car is priced at §22,000. If you make a §4,000 down payment, how much will you finance with a loan?","a": "§18,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§18,000","§20,000","§16,000","§26,000"],"steps": ["The financed loan amount is the price minus the down payment.","Set up §22,000 − §4,000.","Subtract carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-03","std": "MM.8","q": "An auto loan requires monthly payments of §420 for 60 months. What is the total of all monthly payments?","a": "§25,200","lvl": 1,"choices": ["§24,000","§25,200","§21,000","§25,620"],"steps": ["Multiply the monthly payment by the number of months.","Set up §420 × 60.","Find the total and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-04","std": "MM.8","q": "A 3-year car lease allows 12,000 miles per year and charges §0.25 per excess mile. If the driver puts 40,000 miles on the car over 3 years, what is the excess mileage fee?","a": "§1,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§400","§1,600","§1,000","§7,000"],"steps": ["Find allowed miles for 3 years, then find the extra miles driven.","Multiply (40,000 − 12,000 × 3) by §0.25.","Finish the math and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-05","std": "MM.8","q": "A car can be bought for §25,000 cash or financed with §3,000 down and §450/month for 60 months. How much extra does financing cost compared to paying cash?","a": "§5,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§2,000","§8,000","§3,000","§5,000"],"steps": ["Find the total financed cost, then subtract the §25,000 cash price.","Set up §3,000 + (§450 × 60) − §25,000.","Simplify in order and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-06","std": "MM.8","q": "Buying a car for §28,000 cash and selling it after 3 years for §17,000 has a net cost of §11,000. Leasing the same car for 36 months costs §320/month plus §2,500 due at signing. How much does buying with cash save over 3 years?","a": "§3,020","lvl": 2,"choices": ["§2,500","§520","§3,020","§14,020"],"steps": ["Find the total 3-year lease cost, then compare it to §11,000.","Compute (§320 × 36) + §2,500, then subtract §11,000.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-07","std": "MM.8","q": "To finance §20,000, Loan A is 48 months at §460/month and Loan B is 72 months at §330/month. Compare the monthly payment and total cost of Loan B to Loan A.","a": "Loan B is §130/month cheaper but costs §1,680 more in total","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Loan B is §130/month cheaper but costs §3,760 more in total","Loan A and Loan B have the same total cost","Loan B is §130/month cheaper but costs §1,680 more in total","Loan B is §130/month cheaper and costs §1,680 less in total"],"steps": ["Compare both the monthly payments and the full loan totals.","Find §460 − §330 per month, then compare (48 × §460) and (72 × §330).","Find the total cost gap and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-08","std": "MM.8","q": "Option 1: Lease a car for §350/month for 36 months with §2,000 down, then buy it at lease-end for §15,000 cash. Option 2: Buy it immediately with §4,000 down and §500/month for 60 months. Which option costs less in total and by how much?","a": "Option 1 costs §4,400 less","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Option 2 costs §1,400 less","Option 1 costs §4,400 less","Option 2 costs §4,400 less","Option 1 costs §2,400 less"],"steps": ["Add every payment and lump sum for Option 1 and for Option 2.","Compare (§350 × 36 + §2,000 + §15,000) with (§4,000 + §500 × 60).","Find which total is lower, subtract, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.8-09","std": "MM.8","q": "A sales rep drives 18,000 miles a year and will keep a car 7 years. Which option fits best?","a": "Buy, with cash or a low-rate loan; a lease's mileage limits cost too much","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Lease a new car every year to avoid repairs","Lease, because the rep builds equity in the car","Lease, because leases have no mileage limits","Buy, with cash or a low-rate loan; a lease's mileage limits cost too much"],"steps": ["Check how 18,000 miles per year fits typical lease mileage limits.","Weigh owning equity over 7 years against loan interest and cash use.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.9-01","std": "MM.9","q": "A rectangular shipping box is 10 inches long, 6 inches wide, and 4 inches tall. What is its volume?","a": "240 cubic inches","lvl": 1,"choices": ["240 cubic inches","20 cubic inches","120 cubic inches","248 cubic inches"],"steps": ["Volume of a rectangular prism is length × width × height.","Set up V = 10 × 6 × 4.","Multiply all three dimensions and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-02","std": "MM.9","q": "A cylindrical soup can has a radius of 3 cm and a height of 10 cm. What is its volume rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "282.74 cm³","lvl": 1,"choices": ["282.74 cm³","94.25 cm³","314.16 cm³","188.50 cm³"],"steps": ["Use the cylinder volume formula V = π × r² × h.","Plug in r = 3 and h = 10 to get V = π × 3² × 10.","Multiply, round to the nearest hundredth, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-03","std": "MM.9","q": "A cubic storage ottoman has an edge length of 5 cm in a scale prototype. What is its total surface area?","a": "150 cm²","lvl": 1,"choices": ["150 cm²","100 cm²","125 cm²","25 cm²"],"steps": ["A cube has 6 equal square faces, so SA = 6 × s².","Plug in s = 5 to set up SA = 6 × 5².","Square 5 first, multiply by 6, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-04","std": "MM.9","q": "A shipping carton must hold exactly 24 cubic blocks, each with an edge length of 3 inches. What is the minimum interior volume of the carton?","a": "648 cubic inches","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1,728 cubic inches","216 cubic inches","72 cubic inches","648 cubic inches"],"steps": ["Find the volume of one cube, then multiply by 24 blocks.","Set up V = 24 × 3³.","Evaluate the power, multiply, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-05","std": "MM.9","q": "A carpenter builds an open-top rectangular wooden planter that is 4 ft long, 2 ft wide, and 1.5 ft tall. How many square feet of wood are needed for the bottom and four sides?","a": "26 sq ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["26 sq ft","34 sq ft","12 sq ft","18 sq ft"],"steps": ["An open-top box has 1 bottom face and 4 side faces.","Set up (4 × 2) + 2(4 × 1.5) + 2(2 × 1.5).","Find each area, add them up, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-06","std": "MM.9","q": "A cylindrical rainwater tank has a diameter of 6 ft and a height of 8 ft. What is its capacity in cubic feet, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "226.19 ft³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["452.39 ft³","150.80 ft³","904.78 ft³","226.19 ft³"],"steps": ["Divide the diameter by 2 first to get the radius.","Use V = π × r² × h with r = 6 ÷ 2 and h = 8.","Compute, round to the nearest hundredth, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-07","std": "MM.9","q": "A company compares two closed cardboard boxes that both hold 64 cubic inches: Box A is a 4 in x 4 in x 4 in cube, and Box B is an 8 in x 4 in x 2 in prism. Which box uses less cardboard and by how much?","a": "Box A uses 16 sq in less cardboard","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Box A uses 32 sq in less cardboard","Box B uses 16 sq in less cardboard","Both use the same amount of cardboard","Box A uses 16 sq in less cardboard"],"steps": ["Compare total surface area of both closed boxes.","Use SA = 2(lw + lh + wh) for Box A and Box B.","Find which surface area is smaller, subtract, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-08","std": "MM.9","q": "A wheelchair ramp must rise 2 feet with a maximum slope of 1:12 (1 foot of rise for every 12 feet of horizontal run). What is the minimum sloped length of the ramp, rounded to the nearest hundredth of a foot?","a": "24.08 ft","lvl": 3,"choices": ["24.08 ft","26.00 ft","24.00 ft","12.17 ft"],"steps": ["Use the 1:12 slope ratio to find the horizontal run for a 2 ft rise.","Apply the Pythagorean theorem to the rise and run: √{rise² + run²}.","Evaluate the root, round to hundredths, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.9-09","std": "MM.9","q": "A closed cylindrical can must hold 1,000 cm³ with the least metal. Which surface area function should you minimize?","a": "SA = 2πr² + 2000/r, from h = 1000/(πr²)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["SA = πr² × h = 1000","SA = 2πr² + 2000/r, from h = 1000/(πr²)","SA = 2πr² + 1000/r, from h = 1000/r","SA = 2πr + 2000/r², from h = 1000/r²"],"steps": ["Write V = π × r² × h = 1,000 and solve it for h in terms of r.","Substitute h into SA = 2π × r² + 2π × r × h.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.10-01","std": "MM.10","part": "a","q": "Which tool and level of precision is most appropriate for measuring a classroom's wall length to draw a 2D floor plan?","a": "A tape measure to the nearest 1/4 inch","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A car odometer to the nearest 0.1 mile","A 6-inch ruler to the nearest inch","A tape measure to the nearest 1/4 inch","A micrometer to the nearest 0.01 mm"],"steps": ["Match the measuring tool to the size of a classroom wall.","A room needs a tool that measures many feet to small fractions.","Pick the most practical tool and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-02","std": "MM.10","part": "b","q": "A rectangular shed is 12 ft wide with 8 ft vertical walls and a triangular gable roof that rises 4 ft above the walls. In a front elevation drawing, what is the total height from the ground to the roof peak?","a": "12 ft","lvl": 1,"choices": ["8 ft","12 ft","16 ft","24 ft"],"steps": ["Total height from ground to peak adds wall height and roof rise.","Set up 8 + 4 for the front elevation height.","Add the heights and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-03","std": "MM.10","part": "d","q": "An architect needs to give a builder exact wall heights, window placements, and roof slopes for a house. Which 2D visual representation is most appropriate?","a": "Scaled orthographic elevation drawings","lvl": 1,"choices": ["A scatter plot","A freehand perspective sketch","A pie chart","Scaled orthographic elevation drawings"],"steps": ["Think about which 2D drawing shows exact heights and slopes.","Builders use scaled straight-on side or front views, not sketches.","Pick the formal drawing type and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-04","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "A symmetrical gable roof spans a 16 ft wide building (8 ft from wall to center) and rises 6 ft from the wall top to the ridge. What is the slanted length of each rafter?","a": "10 ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["12 ft","17.09 ft","10 ft","14 ft"],"steps": ["The half-span, rise, and rafter form a right triangle.","Use the Pythagorean theorem: rafter = √{8² + 6²}.","Simplify inside the root, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-05","std": "MM.10","part": "b","q": "An L-shaped building has a front wing 20 ft wide and 10 ft tall. Attached to its right side is a taller wing 10 ft wide and 15 ft tall. What are the overall width and maximum height shown in the front elevation drawing?","a": "30 ft wide and 15 ft tall","lvl": 2,"choices": ["30 ft wide and 25 ft tall","20 ft wide and 10 ft tall","20 ft wide and 15 ft tall","30 ft wide and 15 ft tall"],"steps": ["A front elevation shows total side-to-side width and tallest height.","Add widths (20 + 10) and take the taller of 10 ft and 15 ft.","Combine both measures and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-06","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "To include a tree in a site elevation drawing, a student measures its shadow as 15 ft at the same time a 6 ft fence post casts a 3 ft shadow. What is the calculated height of the tree?","a": "30 ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["45 ft","7.5 ft","30 ft","18 ft"],"steps": ["Use similar triangles to set up a proportion of height to shadow.","Set up {h/15} = {6/3} and solve for h.","Finish solving for h and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-07","std": "MM.10","part": "c","q": "A surveyor stands 40 ft from a flagpole and measures an angle of elevation of 35° to the top from an eye height of 5 ft. What is the calculated height of the flagpole, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "33.01 ft","lvl": 3,"choices": ["33.01 ft","37.77 ft","28.01 ft","62.13 ft"],"steps": ["Use tangent to find the height above eye level, then add 5 ft.","Set up height = 40tan(35°) + 5.","Compute in degree mode, round to hundredths, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-08","std": "MM.10","part": "a","q": "A rectangular patio measures 40 ft by 25 ft. If you draw a 2D plan view using a scale of 1 inch = 4 feet, what are the dimensions of the patio on paper?","a": "10 in by 6.25 in","lvl": 3,"choices": ["10 in by 6.25 in","8 in by 5 in","10 in by 5 in","160 in by 100 in"],"steps": ["Divide each real length by 4 feet per inch to get paper inches.","Set up {40/4} and {25/4} for the scaled dimensions.","Simplify both fractions and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.10-09","std": "MM.10","part": "d","q": "Which gives one advantage each for paper orthographic views and 3D CAD software when designing a bookshelf?","a": "Paper: fast and cheap. CAD: easy edits, exact dimensions, fit checks","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Paper: fast and cheap. CAD: easy edits, exact dimensions, fit checks","Both: give the same advantages, so neither is better","Paper: automatic dimensions. CAD: needs no computer","Paper: rotates the model. CAD: cheaper than paper"],"steps": ["Think about speed and cost for paper versus accuracy and edits for CAD.","Give one clear strength for paper views and one for 3D CAD.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.11-01","std": "MM.11","part": "a","q": "A point in 3D space has coordinates (3, −2, 5). How far is this point from the xy-plane?","a": "5 units","lvl": 1,"choices": ["3 units","2 units","5 units","6 units"],"steps": ["The xy-plane is where z = 0, so distance depends on z.","Take the absolute value of the z-coordinate in (3, −2, 5).","Match that distance to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.11-02","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "What is the midpoint of the segment connecting (2, 4, 6) and (6, 8, 10)?","a": "(4, 6, 8)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(8, 12, 16)","(4, 6, 8)","(2, 2, 2)","(3, 6, 8)"],"steps": ["Average the matching x-, y-, and z-coordinates.","Add each pair (2 + 6, 4 + 8, 6 + 10) and divide each sum by 2.","Write the ordered triple and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-03","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "What is the distance between the origin (0, 0, 0) and the point (3, 4, 0)?","a": "5 units","lvl": 1,"choices": ["25 units","12 units","5 units","7 units"],"steps": ["Square each coordinate difference, add them, and take the square root.","Set up d = √{3² + 4² + 0²}.","Add the squares, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-04","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "In a warehouse coordinate system (in meters), a drone hovers at (1, 2, 3) and a sensor is mounted at (4, 6, 3). What is the straight-line distance between them?","a": "5 meters","lvl": 2,"choices": ["5 meters","25 meters","6 meters","7 meters"],"steps": ["Find the change in x, y, and z between the two points.","Set up d = √{(4 − 1)² + (6 − 2)² + (3 − 3)²}.","Simplify inside the root, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-05","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "A support cable runs from anchor A at (−2, 5, 8) to anchor B at (6, −1, 4). Where should a central bracket be placed at the exact midpoint?","a": "(2, 2, 6)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(2, 2, 6)","(4, 3, 2)","(2, 3, 6)","(4, 4, 12)"],"steps": ["Average the x-, y-, and z-values of both anchor points.","Add (−2 + 6), (5 + (−1)), and (8 + 4), then divide each by 2.","Form the midpoint triple and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-06","std": "MM.11","part": "b","q": "A right rectangular prism in Octant I has one vertex at the origin (0, 0, 0), edges along the positive x-, y-, and z-axes, and its opposite vertex at (4, 3, 5). What is the volume of the prism?","a": "60 cubic units","lvl": 2,"choices": ["60 cubic units","12 cubic units","94 cubic units","47 cubic units"],"steps": ["The opposite corner (4, 3, 5) gives the prism's length, width, height.","Set up V = 4 × 3 × 5.","Multiply the three edge lengths and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-07","std": "MM.11","part": "c","q": "A zip line starts at a platform at (2, −1, 4) and ends at a tower at (5, 3, 16), where coordinates are in meters. What is the length of the zip line?","a": "13 meters","lvl": 3,"choices": ["15 meters","169 meters","19 meters","13 meters"],"steps": ["Use the 3D distance formula on the x, y, and z differences.","Set up d = √{(5 − 2)² + (3 − (−1))² + (16 − 4)²}.","Square, add, take the root, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-08","std": "MM.11","part": "d","q": "A structural beam has one endpoint at A(1, −3, 2) and its midpoint at M(4, 1, 5). What are the coordinates of the other endpoint B?","a": "(7, 5, 8)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(2.5, −1, 3.5)","(7, 5, 8)","(5, −2, 7)","(7, −1, 8)"],"steps": ["To find the other endpoint, double each midpoint value and subtract A.","Compute 2(4) − 1 for x, 2(1) − (−3) for y, and 2(5) − 2 for z.","Check your triple by averaging it with A, then match the choices."]},{"id": "MM.11-09","std": "MM.11","part": "b","q": "P(−3, 4, 2) is a vertex of a box with one vertex at the origin and edges on the axes. Which axis vertices does the box use, and where is P?","a": "(−3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant II","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant I","(0, −3, 0), (4, 0, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant II","(−3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant II","(−3, 0, 0), (0, 4, 0), (0, 0, 2); Octant IV"],"steps": ["Start at (0, 0, 0) and move along each axis by the signed coordinates.","Name the three axis vertices, complete the box to P, and note signs.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.12-01","std": "MM.12","q": "A 3D-printed bracket corner at (−4, 2, 7) is translated 6 units in the positive x-direction, 5 units in the negative y-direction, and 3 units in the positive z-direction. What are its new coordinates?","a": "(2, −3, 10)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(2, 7, 10)","(2, −3, 10)","(2, −3, 4)","(−10, 7, 4)"],"steps": ["Add each shift to the matching x-, y-, and z-coordinate.","Set up (−4 + 6, 2 − 5, 7 + 3).","Simplify each coordinate and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-02","std": "MM.12","q": "A drone waypoint at (−5, 8, −2) is reflected across the xz-plane. What are the coordinates of the reflected waypoint?","a": "(−5, −8, −2)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(5, 8, −2)","(−5, −8, −2)","(5, −8, 2)","(−5, 8, 2)"],"steps": ["Reflecting across the xz-plane flips only the y-coordinate's sign.","Keep x = −5 and z = −2 the same, and change 8 to its opposite.","Write the new ordered triple and check it against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-03","std": "MM.12","q": "A sculpture vertex at (6, −9, 12) is dilated from the origin by a scale factor of 1/3. What are the new coordinates?","a": "(2, −3, 4)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(2, 3, 4)","(3, −6, 9)","(18, −27, 36)","(2, −3, 4)"],"steps": ["Multiply every coordinate by the scale factor {1/3}.","Set up (6 × {1/3}, −9 × {1/3}, 12 × {1/3}).","Simplify each value and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-04","std": "MM.12","q": "A camera sensor at (0, 5, −3) is rotated 90° counterclockwise about the x-axis (viewed from the positive x-axis, sending (x, y, z) to (x, −z, y)). What are its new coordinates?","a": "(0, 3, 5)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(0, −5, −3)","(5, 0, −3)","(0, −3, −5)","(0, 3, 5)"],"steps": ["Apply the given rotation rule (x, y, z) to (x, −z, y).","Plug in x = 0, y = 5, and z = −3 to get (0, −(−3), 5).","Simplify the signs carefully and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-05","std": "MM.12","q": "A furniture corner at (3, −2, 4) is dilated from the origin by a scale factor of 2 and then translated by (−1, 4, −5). What are the final coordinates?","a": "(5, 0, 3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(5, 0, 3)","(4, 4, −2)","(7, −8, 13)","(5, 2, 3)"],"steps": ["Do the transformations in order: dilate first, then translate.","Multiply (3, −2, 4) by 2, then add (−1, 4, −5) to the result.","Simplify all three coordinates and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-06","std": "MM.12","q": "A solar panel corner at (4, −1, 3) is rotated 180° about the y-axis. What are its new coordinates?","a": "(−4, −1, −3)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(4, 1, −3)","(−4, −1, −3)","(−4, −1, 3)","(−4, 1, −3)"],"steps": ["A 180° turn about the y-axis keeps y and flips x and z.","Apply (x, y, z) to (−x, y, −z) on the point (4, −1, 3).","Write the new triple and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-07","std": "MM.12","q": "A point at (4, 2, −1) is rotated 270° counterclockwise about the z-axis (sending (x, y, z) to (y, −x, z)) and then reflected across the yz-plane. What are the resulting coordinates?","a": "(−2, −4, −1)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["(−2, 4, 1)","(−2, −4, −1)","(2, 4, −1)","(2, −4, −1)"],"steps": ["Apply the z-axis rotation first, then the yz-plane reflection.","Send (4, 2, −1) to (y, −x, z), then flip the new x-coordinate.","Verify each sign step by step and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-08","std": "MM.12","q": "A rectangular prism has a surface area of 54 sq cm. After a dilation from the origin with a scale factor of 2.5, what is the surface area of the enlarged prism?","a": "337.5 sq cm","lvl": 3,"choices": ["843.75 sq cm","135 sq cm","270 sq cm","337.5 sq cm"],"steps": ["Surface area scales by the square of the scale factor, k².","Set up 54 × (2.5)² to find the enlarged surface area.","Square 2.5 first, multiply by 54, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.12-09","std": "MM.12","q": "A game developer builds the left pillar of an archway (x < 0, centered on the z-axis). Which moves make the right pillar and then shift the archway 10 units along the positive y-axis?","a": "Reflect across the yz-plane, then translate every point by (0, 10, 0)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Reflect across the xz-plane, then translate every point by (0, 10, 0)","Reflect across the yz-plane, then translate every point by (10, 0, 0)","Rotate 90° about the z-axis, then translate by (0, 0, 10)","Reflect across the yz-plane, then translate every point by (0, 10, 0)"],"steps": ["Choose the reflection plane that mirrors x < 0 across to x > 0.","Write the coordinate rule for that reflection and a +10 y-shift.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.13-01","std": "MM.13","q": "A natural history museum builds a 1:40 scale model of a 12-meter-long whale skeleton. How long is the model in centimeters?","a": "30 cm","lvl": 1,"choices": ["30 cm","48 cm","3 cm","300 cm"],"steps": ["Convert 12 meters to centimeters first (1 m = 100 cm).","Divide the centimeter length by 40: (12 × 100) ÷ 40.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-02","std": "MM.13","q": "A landscape architect's model uses the scale 1 inch = 15 feet. A pond measures 3.5 inches wide on the model. What is the actual width of the pond?","a": "52.5 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["60 feet","45 feet","52.5 feet","18.5 feet"],"steps": ["Multiply the model width in inches by 15 feet per inch.","Set up 3.5 × 15 to find the actual width in feet.","Finish multiplying and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-03","std": "MM.13","q": "An architectural model of a 90-foot water tower is 18 inches tall. What is the scale of the model?","a": "1 inch = 5 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1 inch = 18 feet","1 inch = 6 feet","1 inch = 4 feet","1 inch = 5 feet"],"steps": ["Divide actual feet by model inches to find feet per 1 inch.","Set up 90 ÷ 18 to find how many feet equal 1 inch.","Write the scale as 1 inch = __ feet and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-04","std": "MM.13","q": "To model a barn whose peak cannot be measured directly, a student measures a 12-foot horizontal run from the outer wall to the center ridge and a 45° roof pitch above a 16-foot vertical wall. How tall is the barn on a 1 inch = 4 feet scale model?","a": "7 inches","lvl": 2,"choices": ["6 inches","4 inches","28 inches","7 inches"],"steps": ["Find the roof rise with trig, add the wall height, then scale.","Use rise = 12tan(45°), add 16 ft, then divide by 4 ft per in.","Finish the division and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-05","std": "MM.13","q": "A 1:5 scale prototype of an industrial fuel tank holds 4 liters of water. How many liters will the full-size tank hold?","a": "500 liters","lvl": 2,"choices": ["250 liters","500 liters","20 liters","100 liters"],"steps": ["Volume scales by the cube of the linear scale factor, k³.","Multiply the prototype volume by 5³: 4 × 5³.","Compute the cube first, multiply by 4, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-06","std": "MM.13","q": "A 1:12 scale dollhouse requires 3 square feet of paint to cover its exterior walls. How many square feet of paint are needed for the full-size house?","a": "432 sq ft","lvl": 2,"choices": ["432 sq ft","36 sq ft","5,184 sq ft","144 sq ft"],"steps": ["Area scales by the square of the linear scale factor, k².","Multiply the model paint area by 12²: 3 × 12².","Square 12 first, multiply by 3, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-07","std": "MM.13","q": "A stadium dome is a hemisphere with a radius of 50 feet. A 1:100 scale 3D-printed model is created. What is the volume of the model dome in cubic inches, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "452.39 in³","lvl": 3,"choices": ["113.10 in³","226.19 in³","452.39 in³","904.78 in³"],"steps": ["Scale the 50 ft radius by {1/100} and convert it to inches.","Use the hemisphere volume formula V = {2/3} × π × r³.","Compute, round to hundredths, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-08","std": "MM.13","q": "On a 1:50 scale model of a courtyard centered at the origin, one corner lamppost is at (6, 4, 0) inches and a second is placed by reflecting it across the yz-plane to (−6, 4, 0) inches. What is the actual distance between these two lampposts in feet?","a": "50 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["50 feet","25 feet","600 feet","100 feet"],"steps": ["Find the model distance between (6, 4, 0) and (−6, 4, 0).","Multiply that distance in inches by 50, then divide by 12.","Simplify to feet and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.13-09","std": "MM.13","q": "A team builds a 1:100 scale model of a lighthouse (cylinder tower, cone roof, attached cottage). Which plan uses indirect measurement and the transformations correctly?","a": "Find the height with shadows or trig; dilate by 1/100; move pieces into place","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Measure the height with a tape measure; dilate by 100","Find the height with trig; dilate by 1/10; skip the other moves","Guess the height; translate the roof down onto the cottage","Find the height with shadows or trig; dilate by 1/100; move pieces into place"],"steps": ["Pick a hard-to-reach length, like height, using shadows or trig.","Connect dilation ({1/100}) to sizing and shifts and turns to assembly.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.14-01","std": "MM.14","q": "A cyclist rides at a steady speed of 14 miles per hour. How far does the cyclist travel in 3.5 hours?","a": "49 miles","lvl": 1,"choices": ["17.5 miles","49 miles","56 miles","42 miles"],"steps": ["Distance equals rate multiplied by time: d = r × t.","Set up d = 14 × 3.5.","Multiply carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-02","std": "MM.14","q": "A video starts with 500 views and triples every day. How many views does it have after 2 days?","a": "4,500 views","lvl": 1,"choices": ["1,500 views","4,500 views","13,500 views","3,000 views"],"steps": ["Tripling each day means multiplying by 3 for each day.","Set up 500 × 3² for 2 days of growth.","Evaluate the power first, multiply, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-03","std": "MM.14","q": "A pendulum completes one full back-and-forth swing every 4 seconds. How many full cycles does it complete in 1 minute?","a": "15 cycles","lvl": 1,"choices": ["24 cycles","12 cycles","15 cycles","240 cycles"],"steps": ["Convert 1 minute to 60 seconds so the time units match.","Divide total seconds by seconds per cycle: 60 ÷ 4.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-04","std": "MM.14","q": "After water treatment, a pond loses 10% of its algae mass each week. If the pond starts with 800 kg of algae, how much algae remains after 3 weeks?","a": "583.2 kg","lvl": 2,"choices": ["648.0 kg","240.0 kg","560.0 kg","583.2 kg"],"steps": ["Losing 10% leaves 90%, so the weekly decay factor is 0.90.","Set up A = 800 × (0.90)³.","Evaluate the power, multiply by 800, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-05","std": "MM.14","q": "A drone's altitude in feet is modeled by h(t) = −4t² + 24t + 10, where t is time in seconds. What is the drone's maximum altitude?","a": "46 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["34 feet","82 feet","46 feet","58 feet"],"steps": ["The maximum of a downward parabola occurs at its vertex.","Find t = {−b/2a} = {−24/(2 × −4)}, then plug t into h(t).","Evaluate h(t) carefully and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-06","std": "MM.14","q": "Water depth at a coastal pier cycles sinusoidally between a low tide of 4 feet and a high tide of 14 feet. What are the midline and amplitude of the depth model?","a": "Midline 9 feet, amplitude 5 feet","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Midline 10 feet, amplitude 5 feet","Midline 7 feet, amplitude 4 feet","Midline 9 feet, amplitude 10 feet","Midline 9 feet, amplitude 5 feet"],"steps": ["Midline is the average of max and min; amplitude is half the gap.","Compute {(14 + 4)/2} for midline and {(14 − 4)/2} for amplitude.","Pair both results and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-07","std": "MM.14","q": "A patient takes a 400 mg dose of medication that decreases by 25% every 6 hours. How much medication remains in the bloodstream after 18 hours?","a": "168.75 mg","lvl": 3,"choices": ["100.00 mg","168.75 mg","126.56 mg","225.00 mg"],"steps": ["Find how many 6-hour periods fit in 18 hours and the decay factor.","Set up 400 × (1 − 0.25)^{n} using n = 18 ÷ 6.","Evaluate carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-08","std": "MM.14","q": "A wind turbine blade tip reaches a maximum height of 95 meters and a minimum height of 25 meters, completing one full rotation every 6 seconds. If the blade tip starts at its maximum height at t = 0, which function models its height h(t)?","a": "h(t) = 35cos((π/3)t) + 60","lvl": 3,"choices": ["h(t) = 35cos((π/3)t) + 60","h(t) = 35cos(6t) + 60","h(t) = 35sin((π/3)t) + 60","h(t) = 70cos((π/3)t) + 60"],"steps": ["Starting at a maximum points to a positive cosine model.","Find amp {(95 − 25)/2}, mid {(95 + 25)/2}, and b = {2π/6}.","Put A, b, and d into A×cos(bt) + d and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.14-09","std": "MM.14","q": "A rabbit population on an island was modeled by P(t) = 50(1.3)^t, but after 10 years the actual population is far lower. Why, and how should the model be revised?","a": "It ignored carrying capacity; switch to a logistic model that levels off","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The model is fine; the data must be wrong","Rabbit populations always decrease; use a decay model","The growth factor was too small; use 1.5 instead","It ignored carrying capacity; switch to a logistic model that levels off"],"steps": ["Spot the hidden assumption in an exponential growth model.","Link limited food and space to carrying capacity and logistic growth.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.15-01","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot shows that as average outdoor temperature increases, a home's monthly heating bill decreases steadily along a straight line. How is this relationship described?","a": "Negative linear association","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Exponential growth","Positive linear association","Negative linear association","No association"],"steps": ["Check the shape (straight line) and direction (downward).","One value rising as the other falls means a negative trend.","Combine shape and direction, then check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-02","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "A linear regression equation for a maple tree's height y (in feet) after x years is y = 1.6x + 3. What is the predicted height after 10 years?","a": "19 feet","lvl": 1,"choices": ["31.6 feet","13 feet","16 feet","19 feet"],"steps": ["Substitute x = 10 into the regression equation.","Set up y = 1.6 × 10 + 3.","Multiply first, then add, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-03","std": "MM.15","part": "a","q": "When creating a scatter plot to model how a car's age affects its resale price, which variable belongs on the horizontal (x) axis?","a": "Car age","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Resale price","Car color","Either variable, since both are responses","Car age"],"steps": ["The explanatory (independent) variable goes on the x-axis.","Ask which variable predicts or explains the other.","Match the explanatory variable to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.15-04","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot of a mug of hot coffee's temperature over time drops rapidly at first and then levels off near room temperature (decreasing and concave up). Which regression model best fits this pattern?","a": "Exponential decay","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Sinusoidal","Linear","Exponential decay","Exponential growth"],"steps": ["Look at how the rate of decrease changes over time.","Falling fast then flattening toward a floor fits an asymptote.","Match that curve behavior to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.15-05","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "Graphing technology yields the exponential regression y = 120 × (1.25)^x for a club's newsletter subscribers y after x weeks. What is the predicted number of subscribers at week 3, rounded to the nearest whole number?","a": "234 subscribers","lvl": 2,"choices": ["234 subscribers","450 subscribers","210 subscribers","188 subscribers"],"steps": ["Plug x = 3 into the exponential regression model.","Set up y = 120 × (1.25)³.","Cube 1.25 first, multiply, round, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-06","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A quadratic regression of monthly profit (y) versus ticket price (x) rises to a single peak at (45, 18000) and then falls. What does (45, 18000) represent?","a": "A ticket price of §45 gives the maximum monthly profit of §18,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A ticket price of §45 gives the maximum monthly profit of §18,000","The company must sell 18,000 tickets to break even","Profit increases by §45 for every §18,000 in sales","At §45 per ticket, the profit drops to zero"],"steps": ["Match each coordinate to its axis: x is price, y is profit.","Read a single peak as the maximum y-value at that x-value.","Interpret (45, 18000) in context and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-07","std": "MM.15","part": "c","q": "A quadratic regression for a car's stopping distance y (in feet) at speed x (in mph) is y = 0.05x² + 1.2x. How much longer is the predicted stopping distance at 60 mph than at 40 mph?","a": "124 feet","lvl": 3,"choices": ["124 feet","100 feet","24 feet","252 feet"],"steps": ["Evaluate the model at both speeds, then find the difference.","Compute y(60) and y(40) using 0.05x² + 1.2x, then subtract.","Finish the subtraction and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.15-08","std": "MM.15","part": "b","q": "A scatter plot of smartphone adoption over 15 years is S-shaped: concave up from year 0 to year 6, and concave down from year 6 to year 15 as it approaches 95%. What is the feature at year 6?","a": "An inflection point where the rate of adoption is fastest","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A horizontal asymptote","An inflection point where the rate of adoption is fastest","A local maximum where adoption stops","A local minimum"],"steps": ["Recall the name for a point where concavity switches.","On an S-curve, the concavity switch marks the steepest growth.","Match that feature and meaning to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.15-09","std": "MM.15","part": "a","q": "A student models a year of electricity use with only the January through April bills (r² = 0.98). Why is this not enough data?","a": "It misses summer peaks; collect 12 to 24 straight months","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Electricity use does not change during the year","Four points are too many for a linear model","r² = 0.98 proves the model fits the whole year","It misses summer peaks; collect 12 to 24 straight months"],"steps": ["Think about how electricity use changes across all four seasons.","Note what Jan–Apr misses and how many months show a full cycle.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.16-01","std": "MM.16","q": "In a linearized model using common logarithms, log(y) = 3. What is the value of y?","a": "1,000","lvl": 1,"choices": ["3","1,000","30","100"],"steps": ["A common log uses base 10, so log(y) = 3 rewrites as a power.","Convert the log equation to y = 10³.","Evaluate the power of 10 and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-02","std": "MM.16","q": "Which coordinate transformation linearizes a power function of the form y = a × x^b?","a": "Plot log(x) versus log(y)","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Plot x² versus y","Plot log(x) versus y","Plot x versus log(y)","Plot log(x) versus log(y)"],"steps": ["Take log of both sides of y = a × x^{b} to see what becomes linear.","Rewrite as log(y) = b × log(x) + log(a) to spot the new axes.","Match those two transformed variables to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.16-03","std": "MM.16","q": "In a model linearized with natural logarithms, ln(y) = 0. What is the value of y?","a": "1","lvl": 1,"choices": ["2.72","10","0","1"],"steps": ["The natural log ln(y) has base e.","Rewrite ln(y) = 0 in exponential form: y = e⁰.","Use the zero-exponent rule and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-04","std": "MM.16","q": "A linearized exponential dataset produces the equation log(y) = 0.25x + 1.5. What is the predicted value of y when x = 6?","a": "1,000","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1,000","300","3","100"],"steps": ["Plug x = 6 into the linear equation to find log(y) first.","Compute 0.25 × 6 + 1.5, then rewrite log(y) as y = 10^{value}.","Evaluate the power of 10 and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-05","std": "MM.16","q": "A lab experiment linearized with natural logarithms gives ln(y) = 0.5x + 2. What is the predicted value of y when x = 4, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "54.60","lvl": 2,"choices": ["10,000.00","20.09","54.60","4.00"],"steps": ["Substitute x = 4 to find ln(y), then convert using base e.","Compute 0.5 × 4 + 2, then set up y = e^{value}.","Evaluate with a calculator, round, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-06","std": "MM.16","q": "Linearizing a power dataset gives log(y) = 3log(x) + 0.699. Given that 10^{0.699} is approximately 5, rewrite this relationship in nonlinear form.","a": "y = 5 × x³","lvl": 2,"choices": ["y = 5 × 3^x","y = 5 × x³","y = 0.699 × x³","y = 3 × x⁵"],"steps": ["Match log(y) = b × log(x) + log(a) to a power model y = a × x^{b}.","Use 10^{0.699} for a and the slope 3 for the exponent b.","Write y = a × x^{b} and check your equation against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-07","std": "MM.16","q": "A scatter plot of x versus log(y) forms a straight line passing through (0, 2) and (4, 3). What is the original exponential equation y = a × b^x, with b rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "y = 100 × (1.78)^x","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 2 × (0.25)^x","y = 100 × (1.78)^x","y = 20 × (1.78)^x","y = 100 × (0.25)^x"],"steps": ["Find the line's slope and y-intercept for log(y) = mx + c.","Convert back with a = 10^{c} and b = 10^{m} using m = {(3 − 2)/(4 − 0)}.","Round b to hundredths and check y = a × b^{x} in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-08","std": "MM.16","q": "A physicist plots x versus log(y) and sees a clear curve in the residuals, but plotting log(x) versus log(y) gives a straight line with slope 1.5 and random residuals. What type of function best models the original data?","a": "A power function of the form y = a × x^{1.5}","lvl": 3,"choices": ["An exponential function of the form y = a × (1.5)^x","A linear function with slope 1.5","A power function of the form y = a × x^{1.5}","A logarithmic function y = 1.5log(x)"],"steps": ["Identify which transformation made the plot straight.","A straight log(x) vs log(y) plot means y = a × x^{b} with slope b.","Insert the given slope as the exponent and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.16-09","std": "MM.16","q": "Radioactive decay data linearizes to log(y) = −0.301t + 2.602 (t in hours). With 10^{−0.301} ≈ 0.5 and 10^{2.602} ≈ 400, what is the exponential model?","a": "y = 400(0.5)^t: 400 g at the start, half left each hour","lvl": 3,"choices": ["y = 400(0.5)^t: 400 g at the start, half left each hour","y = 400(0.301)^t: 400 g, losing 30.1% each hour","y = 0.5(400)^t: 0.5 g, growing each hour","y = 2.602(0.301)^t: 2.6 g at the start"],"steps": ["Use base 10 on both sides to rewrite log(y) = mt + c as a × b^{t}.","Substitute the given powers of 10 for initial mass a and factor b.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.17-01","std": "MM.17","q": "Which of the following is NOT a statistical question?","a": "What is the current population of Birmingham, Alabama?","lvl": 1,"choices": ["What percentage of Alabama drivers wear seatbelts?","How many miles do teachers at our school commute each day?","How much do 10th graders spend on lunch each week?","What is the current population of Birmingham, Alabama?"],"steps": ["A statistical question expects variability across many data values.","Look for the question with one fixed factual answer.","Pick the non-statistical question and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.17-02","std": "MM.17","q": "In the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle, what is the final step after analyzing the collected data?","a": "Interpret the results in the context of the original question","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Remove any data points that disagree with the hypothesis","Formulate a statistical question","Collect a second dataset","Interpret the results in the context of the original question"],"steps": ["Think about what comes after collecting and analyzing data.","After calculations finish, connect findings back to the question.","Match that final phase to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.17-03","std": "MM.17","q": "A researcher records whether students who choose to eat breakfast earn higher math grades, without assigning anyone what to eat. What type of study is this?","a": "An observational study","lvl": 1,"choices": ["An observational study","A randomized experiment","A simulation","A double-blind clinical trial"],"steps": ["Check whether the researcher assigned treatments or just watched.","Recording choices people already made means no treatment imposed.","Match that study type to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.17-04","std": "MM.17","q": "A principal wants to estimate the average daily screen time of all 1,200 students at her school. She randomly selects 80 students from the school roster to survey. What are the population and the sample?","a": "Population: all 1,200 students; Sample: the 80 selected students","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Population: the 80 selected students; Sample: all 1,200 students","Population: daily screen time; Sample: 80 hours","Population: all 1,200 students; Sample: the 80 selected students","Population: all high schoolers in the state; Sample: all 1,200 students"],"steps": ["Population is the full group; sample is the smaller group surveyed.","Match the full school roster to population and the 80 to sample.","Confirm both labels in order and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.17-05","std": "MM.17","q": "A bakery tests whether a new yeast makes bread rise higher by randomly assigning 20 dough batches to the new yeast and 20 to standard yeast, baking all loaves in the same oven at 375°F. Why is using the same oven temperature important?","a": "It controls a potential confounding variable","lvl": 2,"choices": ["It increases the margin of error","It eliminates the need for a control group","It creates a simple random sample","It controls a potential confounding variable"],"steps": ["Ask what could happen if one yeast group baked in a hotter oven.","Keeping temperature identical isolates the effect of the yeast.","Match that design purpose to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.17-06","std": "MM.17","q": "A study randomly assigns 100 office volunteers to use standing desks or sitting desks for 3 months and finds significantly less back pain in the standing desk group. What conclusion is justified?","a": "Standing desks caused lower back pain for these 100 office volunteers","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Standing desks caused lower back pain for these 100 office volunteers","Only correlation, not causation, can be concluded","Standing desks cure back pain for every person in the world","The results are invalid because volunteers were used"],"steps": ["Check if the study used random assignment and random sampling.","Random assignment tests cause; random sampling allows generalizing.","Match what this study can claim to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.17-07","std": "MM.17","q": "A health study tracks 5,000 people who already drink green tea daily and 5,000 who do not, finding lower heart disease rates among tea drinkers. Why can researchers NOT conclude that green tea prevents heart disease?","a": "It is an observational study; tea drinkers may also exercise more or eat healthier (confounding variables)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Heart disease cannot be measured as a categorical variable","It is an observational study; tea drinkers may also exercise more or eat healthier (confounding variables)","Observational studies can never identify associations","The sample size of 10,000 is too small"],"steps": ["Decide if people chose to drink tea or were randomly assigned.","Without random assignment, other health habits can explain results.","Match that reasoning to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.17-08","std": "MM.17","q": "A school district wants to estimate parent approval of a new dress code across 5 schools of different sizes. Why is a stratified random sample by school better than surveying parents who attend a PTA meeting?","a": "A stratified random sample represents all 5 schools without voluntary response bias, whereas PTA attendees are a biased convenience sample","lvl": 3,"choices": ["A stratified random sample guarantees 100% approval","A stratified random sample represents all 5 schools without voluntary response bias, whereas PTA attendees are a biased convenience sample","Stratified sampling turns the survey into a randomized experiment","PTA meetings have too many parents to count"],"steps": ["Compare who shows up to a PTA meeting versus all district parents.","Stratifying by school ensures every school is fairly represented.","Match that bias comparison to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.17-09","std": "MM.17","q": "Which plan uses all four phases of the Statistical Problem-Solving Cycle to learn whether adults support Sunday library hours?","a": "Ask a question, survey a random sample, find the proportion, interpret","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Ask a question, survey a random sample, find the proportion, interpret","Survey 400 adults at the library on Sunday","Post a library poll and report the votes","Ask the library staff and report their opinion"],"steps": ["List the four phases: formulate, collect, analyze, and interpret.","Plan a random sample, neutral question, and proportion estimate.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.18-01","std": "MM.18","part": "a","q": "A weather station records measurable rain on 18 out of 60 spring days. What is the empirical probability of rain on a spring day?","a": "0.30","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.18","0.30","0.60","0.42"],"steps": ["Empirical probability is observed successes divided by total trials.","Set up {18/60} and convert the fraction to a decimal.","Finish dividing and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-02","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "In a cumulative relative frequency graph (ogive), what height does the curve always reach at the largest data value?","a": "1","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0","1","0.5","The mean of the distribution"],"steps": ["Cumulative relative frequency adds proportions up to each value.","At the largest value, 100% of the data has been included.","Write 100% as a proportion and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-03","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "Which measures of center and spread are most appropriate for a roughly symmetric, bell-shaped probability distribution with no outliers?","a": "Mean and standard deviation","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Mode and range","Mean and standard deviation","Median and interquartile range (IQR)","Median and range"],"steps": ["Match center and spread measures to the distribution's shape.","When there are no outliers or skew, non-resistant measures work best.","Pick the pair best for symmetric data and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-04","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "An empirical probability distribution for the number of cars waiting at a coffee drive-thru is: 0 cars (0.1), 1 car (0.3), 2 cars (0.4), 3 cars (0.2). What is the mean number of cars waiting?","a": "1.7 cars","lvl": 2,"choices": ["1.5 cars","1.7 cars","2.0 cars","1.2 cars"],"steps": ["The mean of a discrete distribution sums each value × probability.","Set up 0(0.1) + 1(0.3) + 2(0.4) + 3(0.2).","Multiply each pair, add them up, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-05","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "The relative frequencies for a class's quiz scores are: 70 (0.10), 80 (0.25), 90 (0.45), and 100 (0.20). What is the cumulative relative frequency at a score of 80?","a": "0.35","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.25","0.35","0.65","0.80"],"steps": ["Cumulative relative frequency adds all frequencies up to 80.","Add the relative frequencies for 70 and 80: 0.10 + 0.25.","Finish adding and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-06","std": "MM.18","part": "a","q": "A sales simulation runs 250 customer calls and 45 result in a purchase. Based on this empirical probability, how many purchases are expected in 1,000 calls?","a": "180 purchases","lvl": 2,"choices": ["180 purchases","125 purchases","45 purchases","225 purchases"],"steps": ["Find the empirical probability, then multiply by 1,000 calls.","Set up {45/250} × 1,000.","Simplify the product and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-07","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "A discrete probability distribution has values X = 0 (p = 0.3), X = 2 (p = 0.4), and X = 4 (p = 0.3), with mean 2. What is the standard deviation of X, rounded to the nearest hundredth?","a": "1.55","lvl": 3,"choices": ["1.20","1.55","2.00","2.40"],"steps": ["Find variance by summing (X − mean)² × p, then take its root.","Compute √{(0 − 2)²(0.3) + (2 − 2)²(0.4) + (4 − 2)²(0.3)}.","Take the square root, round to hundredths, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-08","std": "MM.18","part": "c","q": "A charity raffle sells tickets for §5 each. The payout probabilities per ticket are: §0 (0.85), §10 (0.10), and §50 (0.05). What is a player's expected net gain or loss per ticket?","a": "Expected loss of §1.50","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Expected gain of §1.50","Expected loss of §1.50","Expected gain of §3.50","Expected loss of §3.50"],"steps": ["Find the expected payout first, then subtract the §5 ticket cost.","Compute 0(0.85) + 10(0.10) + 50(0.05), then subtract 5.","Interpret the sign as gain or loss and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.18-09","std": "MM.18","part": "b","q": "Over 100 days a website had 0 outages on 72 days, 1 on 18, 2 on 7 and 3 on 3. Which describes the distribution?","a": "Right-skewed, mean 0.41; the median of 0 better shows a typical day","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Right-skewed, mean 1; the median of 1 shows a typical day","Symmetric, mean 1.5; the mean shows a typical day","Right-skewed, mean 0.41; the median of 0 better shows a typical day","Left-skewed, mean 0.41; the mean shows a typical day"],"steps": ["Check where the peak sits and which direction the tail stretches.","Compute the mean with sum of value × proportion, and find median.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.19-01","std": "MM.19","part": "a","q": "A multiple-choice question has 4 options, so guessing gives a 0.25 probability of being correct. If a student guesses on 2 independent questions, what is the probability of getting both right?","a": "0.0625","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.0625","0.125","0.50","0.25"],"steps": ["For independent events, multiply their probabilities together.","Set up 0.25 × 0.25.","Multiply carefully and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-02","std": "MM.19","part": "c","q": "A population of battery lifetimes has a mean of 40 hours and a standard deviation of 8 hours. For random samples of size n = 16, what is the standard deviation of the sample mean?","a": "2 hours","lvl": 1,"choices": ["0.5 hours","4 hours","2 hours","8 hours"],"steps": ["The standard deviation of the sample mean is σ ÷ √{n}.","Plug in σ = 8 and n = 16 to get 8 ÷ √{16}.","Take the root, divide, and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-03","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "Can a normal approximation be used for the sampling distribution of a sample proportion when n = 40 and p = 0.10?","a": "No, because np = 4 is less than 10","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Yes, because n ≥ 30","No, because np = 4 is less than 10","No, because p is not 0.50","Yes, because n(1 − p) = 36 is at least 10"],"steps": ["Normal approximation requires both np ≥ 10 and n(1 − p) ≥ 10.","Compute 40 × 0.10 and 40 × 0.90 to test both conditions.","See if both reach 10, then check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-04","std": "MM.19","part": "a","q": "A packet of flower seeds has a 70% germination rate. If 3 seeds are planted independently, what is the probability that exactly 2 seeds germinate?","a": "0.441","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.441","0.490","0.343","0.147"],"steps": ["Use the binomial rule: (ways to pick 2 of 3) × p² × (1 − p)¹.","There are 3 orders, so set up 3 × (0.70)² × (0.30).","Evaluate the product and check your answer against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-05","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "In a large city, 36% of households own a dog (p = 0.36). For random samples of size n = 64, what is the standard deviation of the sample proportion?","a": "0.06","lvl": 2,"choices": ["0.0036","0.06","0.045","0.48"],"steps": ["Use the sample proportion SD formula: √{p(1 − p) ÷ n}.","Plug in p = 0.36 and n = 64: √{0.36 × 0.64 ÷ 64}.","Simplify inside the root, take the root, and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-06","std": "MM.19","part": "d","q": "The sampling distribution of a sample mean is approximately normal with mean 120 and standard deviation 5. Using the Empirical Rule, what is the probability a random sample mean falls between 110 and 130?","a": "95%","lvl": 2,"choices": ["99.7%","50%","95%","68%"],"steps": ["Find how many standard deviations 110 and 130 are from 120.","Compute {(130 − 120)/5} and recall the 68-95-99.7 rule.","Match that number of SDs to its percent and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-07","std": "MM.19","part": "c","q": "A right-skewed population of package weights has mean 50 lb and standard deviation 15 lb. For random samples of size n = 225, what is the approximate probability that a sample mean is less than 49 lb?","a": "16% (or 0.159)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["50% (or 0.500)","34% (or 0.341)","16% (or 0.159)","2.5% (or 0.023)"],"steps": ["With n = 225, CLT gives a normal sample mean with SD = 15 ÷ √{225}.","Find how many SDs below 50 the value 49 sits, then find the tail.","Use the 68% middle rule to find the lower tail and check choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-08","std": "MM.19","part": "d","q": "A fair six-sided die is rolled 5 times. What is the probability of rolling at least one 6, rounded to the nearest thousandth?","a": "0.598","lvl": 3,"choices": ["0.833","0.167","0.402","0.598"],"steps": ["Use the complement: 1 minus the chance of rolling zero 6s.","Set up 1 − ({5/6})⁵.","Evaluate, round to thousandths, and check against the choices."]},{"id": "MM.19-09","std": "MM.19","part": "b","q": "Samples of n = 200 chips come from a line with a 10% defect rate. What range of sample proportions is expected about 95% of the time?","a": "About 0.058 to 0.142 (mean 0.10, SD about 0.021)","lvl": 3,"choices": ["About 0.04 to 0.16 (mean 0.10, SD 0.03)","About 0.079 to 0.121 (mean 0.10, SD about 0.021)","About 0.10 to 0.20 (mean 0.15, SD 0.025)","About 0.058 to 0.142 (mean 0.10, SD about 0.021)"],"steps": ["Verify np ≥ 10 and n(1 − p) ≥ 10 using n = 200 and p = 0.10.","Find mean = p and SD = √{p(1 − p) ÷ n}, then form mean ± 2 × SD.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.20-01","std": "MM.20","part": "a","q": "A commuter survey estimates an average commute time of 26 minutes with a margin of error of 2.5 minutes. What is the confidence interval?","a": "(23.5, 28.5) minutes","lvl": 1,"choices": ["(23.5, 26.0) minutes","(23.5, 28.5) minutes","(26.0, 28.5) minutes","(21.0, 31.0) minutes"],"steps": ["A confidence interval is estimate minus ME to estimate plus ME.","Set up 26 − 2.5 for the lower bound and 26 + 2.5 for the upper.","Compute both endpoints and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-02","std": "MM.20","part": "c","q": "A cereal box is labeled 16 ounces. A quality inspector tests whether the filling machine is off-target (either overfilling or underfilling). What are the null and alternative hypotheses?","a": "H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ ≠ 16","lvl": 1,"choices": ["H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ > 16","H0: μ ≠ 16, Ha: μ = 16","H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ ≠ 16","H0: μ = 16, Ha: μ < 16"],"steps": ["The null hypothesis H0 always states equality (no change).","Testing over OR underfilling calls for a two-sided ≠ in Ha.","Match both hypotheses to the choices and check your pick."]},{"id": "MM.20-03","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "A significance test yields a p-value of 0.14 at a significance level of α = 0.05. What is the correct decision?","a": "Fail to reject the null hypothesis","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Prove the null hypothesis is 100% true","Fail to reject the null hypothesis","Lower α to 0.01","Reject the null hypothesis"],"steps": ["Compare the p-value to α: reject H0 only if p-value ≤ α.","Check whether 0.14 is less than or greater than 0.05.","Make the matching decision and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-04","std": "MM.20","part": "a","q": "In a random sample of 100 customers, 60% prefer online billing (p̂ = 0.60, standard error = 0.049). Using a critical value of 1.96, what is the 95% confidence interval rounded to three decimal places?","a": "(0.504, 0.696)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["(0.504, 0.696)","(0.502, 0.698)","(0.551, 0.649)","(0.404, 0.796)"],"steps": ["Multiply the critical value by SE to get the margin of error.","Compute ME = 1.96 × 0.049, then form 0.60 − ME and 0.60 + ME.","Round both bounds to three decimals and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-05","std": "MM.20","part": "b","q": "A 95% confidence interval for the mean wait time at an urgent care clinic is (14.2, 18.8) minutes. Which interpretation is correct?","a": "We are 95% confident that the true population mean wait time is between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Exactly 95% of clinics have wait times in this interval","We are 95% confident that the true population mean wait time is between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes","95% of all patients wait between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes","There is a 95% chance the sample mean is between 14.2 and 18.8 minutes"],"steps": ["A confidence interval estimates the true population parameter.","Eliminate choices about individual patients or the sample mean.","Pick the proper population-mean statement and check choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-06","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "In a hypothesis test, what is a Type II error?","a": "Failing to reject a false null hypothesis","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Rejecting a true null hypothesis","Rejecting a false null hypothesis","Failing to reject a false null hypothesis","Failing to reject a true null hypothesis"],"steps": ["Recall the two decision errors: rejecting true H0 or missing false H0.","Match Type I to a false positive and Type II to a false negative.","Connect Type II to the decision on H0 and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-07","std": "MM.20","part": "c","q": "A manufacturer claims its steel bolts have a mean length of 50 mm. A random sample of 36 bolts has a mean of 49.4 mm and a standard deviation of 1.2 mm. What is the t-test statistic for H0: μ = 50?","a": "−3.0","lvl": 3,"choices": ["−18.0","3.0","−0.5","−3.0"],"steps": ["Use the t-statistic formula: t = (x̄ − μ) ÷ (s ÷ √{n}).","Find SE = 1.2 ÷ √{36}, then divide (49.4 − 50) by that SE.","Keep the negative sign, simplify, and check against choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-08","std": "MM.20","part": "b","q": "A city transit authority will fund a new bus route only if more than 40% of commuters would use it. A random survey produces a 95% confidence interval of (43%, 51%). What strategic decision is supported?","a": "Fund the bus route, because the entire confidence interval lies above 40%","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Fund the bus route, because the entire confidence interval lies above 40%","Do not fund the route, because 51% is barely above half","Wait until the confidence interval includes 40%","No decision can be made because 40% is not inside the interval"],"steps": ["Compare the entire confidence interval to the 40% threshold.","Check whether every plausible value from 43% to 51% beats 40%.","Match that evidence to the decision and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.20-09","std": "MM.20","part": "d","q": "A brake maker tests H0: brakes meet safety standards against Ha: brakes are defective. Which error is more dangerous, and how should α be set?","a": "Type II (missing bad brakes); use a larger α such as 0.10","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Type II (missing bad brakes); use a larger α such as 0.10","Type II (missing bad brakes); use a smaller α such as 0.01","Neither; α should always be 0.05","Type I (recalling good brakes); use a smaller α such as 0.01"],"steps": ["Define Type I (false alarm) and Type II (missed defect) in context.","Weigh safety vs recall cost, then note how larger α cuts Type II.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.21-01","std": "MM.21","q": "A restaurant leaves optional comment cards on tables and reports that 90% of diners are unhappy with service. What type of bias most likely distorts this result?","a": "Voluntary response bias","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Placebo effect","Random sampling error","Cluster bias","Voluntary response bias"],"steps": ["Notice that diners chose on their own whether to respond.","Optional feedback mostly draws people with strong complaints.","Match that self-selection bias to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.21-02","std": "MM.21","q": "A polling firm calls only landline phone numbers to estimate the voting preferences of young adults aged 18 to 25. What type of bias occurs?","a": "Undercoverage bias","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Random error","Undercoverage bias","Wording bias","Response bias"],"steps": ["Ask which part of the 18–25 population is left out by landlines.","Excluding a large group from the sampling frame causes bias.","Pick the bias name for leaving groups out and check choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-03","std": "MM.21","q": "If an online website increases its biased voluntary-response poll from 500 clicks to 50,000 clicks, what happens to the bias?","a": "The bias remains because larger sample sizes do not fix a biased sampling method","lvl": 1,"choices": ["The bias is completely eliminated","The bias is reduced by a factor of 10","The poll becomes a simple random sample","The bias remains because larger sample sizes do not fix a biased sampling method"],"steps": ["Separate sample size (precision) from sampling method (bias).","More clicks shrink random error, not a flawed selection method.","Decide what happens to the bias and check your answer in the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-04","std": "MM.21","q": "A school administrator stands in the hallway and asks students face-to-face if they have ever cheated on an exam. What bias will likely cause the cheating rate to be underestimated?","a": "Response bias (social desirability bias)","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Sampling variability","Nonresponse bias","Undercoverage bias","Response bias (social desirability bias)"],"steps": ["Think about how students answer a sensitive question face-to-face.","People often hide socially frowned-upon behavior from authority.","Match that inaccurate-answering bias to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.21-05","std": "MM.21","q": "A blog claims sparkling water boosts mood based on a 12-person study that found a slight increase with p = 0.18. What is the strongest statistical critique?","a": "The p-value of 0.18 is not statistically significant, so the observed difference could easily be due to random chance","lvl": 2,"choices": ["A p-value must be above 0.50 to show an effect","The p-value of 0.18 is not statistically significant, so the observed difference could easily be due to random chance","Mood cannot be studied in statistics","12 people is always enough to prove causation"],"steps": ["Compare p = 0.18 to a standard significance level like 0.05.","A large p-value means the result could easily happen by chance.","Pick the critique about significance and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-06","std": "MM.21","q": "A toothpaste ad claims '4 out of 5 dentists recommend Brand Z' without stating how many dentists were surveyed or whether they could recommend multiple brands. Why should a consumer be skeptical?","a": "Missing details on sample size, sampling method, and question wording make the claim unverifiable and potentially misleading","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Missing details on sample size, sampling method, and question wording make the claim unverifiable and potentially misleading","Because 4 out of 5 is less than 50%","Because dentists cannot be surveyed statistically","Because 5 dentists is the entire population"],"steps": ["List what a trustworthy survey report must disclose.","Look at what the ad leaves out: sample size, method, and wording.","Pick the critique about missing details and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-07","std": "MM.21","q": "A poll reports Candidate A leads Candidate B by 51% to 49% with a margin of error of ±3%. A headline declares, 'Candidate A Clearly Ahead!' Why is the headline statistically flawed?","a": "The 2-percentage-point difference is well within the ±3% margin of error, making the race a statistical tie","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Candidate A needs at least 75% to be ahead","Polls cannot be used for elections","The 2-percentage-point difference is well within the ±3% margin of error, making the race a statistical tie","A margin of error of ±3% is too large to publish"],"steps": ["Compare the 2% lead to the ±3% margin of error.","Check whether the two candidates' intervals overlap.","Decide if a clear lead is supported and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-08","std": "MM.21","q": "A 10-year observational study finds that adults who buy organic groceries live 3 years longer on average than those who do not, and concludes organic food adds 3 years to your life. Critique this conclusion.","a": "Income, healthcare access, and overall lifestyle are confounding variables in an observational study, so causation cannot be established","lvl": 3,"choices": ["The study suffered from voluntary response bias only","Life expectancy is a categorical variable","10 years is too long for an observational study","Income, healthcare access, and overall lifestyle are confounding variables in an observational study, so causation cannot be established"],"steps": ["Check whether people were randomly assigned to buy organic food.","Think of lifestyle or income factors tied to both food and health.","Decide if causation is justified and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.21-09","std": "MM.21","q": "A phone maker tested 15 battery settings at α = 0.05 and advertised only the one with p = 0.03. Why is the claim weak?","a": "With 15 tests, a false positive is likely (about 54%); it needs replication","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Testing many settings always makes p-values more reliable","p = 0.03 is above 0.05, so it is not significant","15 tests is too few to find any effect","With 15 tests, a false positive is likely (about 54%); it needs replication"],"steps": ["Spot multiple testing (p-hacking) and hiding the 14 null results.","Find the chance of ≥ 1 false positive: 1 − (1 − 0.05)^{15}.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]},{"id": "MM.22-01","std": "MM.22","q": "A student wants to randomly assign 24 volunteers equally into Treatment Group A and Control Group B. Which method is valid?","a": "Write all 24 names on identical slips, mix them in a hat, and draw 12 for Group A and the rest for Group B","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Put boys in Group A and girls in Group B","Write all 24 names on identical slips, mix them in a hat, and draw 12 for Group A and the rest for Group B","Put the first 12 volunteers who arrive into Group A","Let volunteers pick which group they want to join"],"steps": ["Valid random assignment gives every person an equal chance.","Eliminate methods using arrival order, choice, or gender.","Pick the true randomization method and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-02","std": "MM.22","q": "In a randomized experiment testing whether stretching for 5 minutes before a 100-meter sprint improves sprint time, what is the explanatory variable?","a": "Whether the runner stretches before sprinting","lvl": 1,"choices": ["Sprint time in seconds","Whether the runner stretches before sprinting","Number of runners","Length of the track"],"steps": ["The explanatory variable is the treatment you test or vary.","Ask which factor is changed to see its effect on sprint time.","Match the treatment variable to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.22-03","std": "MM.22","q": "When designing a student survey, why is it important to use neutral, non-leading question wording?","a": "To avoid wording bias that pushes respondents toward a specific answer","lvl": 1,"choices": ["To make the sample size larger","To eliminate all random sampling error","To avoid wording bias that pushes respondents toward a specific answer","To turn the survey into an experiment"],"steps": ["Think about how loaded words can nudge people's answers.","Neutral phrasing keeps responses honest rather than steered.","Match that reason to the choices and check your answer."]},{"id": "MM.22-04","std": "MM.22","q": "A student wants to sample students from a high school with 40 homerooms. She randomly selects 2 homerooms and surveys every student in those 2 rooms. What sampling method is this?","a": "Cluster sampling","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Simple random sampling","Cluster sampling","Voluntary response sampling","Stratified random sampling"],"steps": ["Notice that whole groups (homerooms) are picked at random.","Distinguish taking some from every group vs all from a few groups.","Match taking all students from 2 random rooms to the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-05","std": "MM.22","q": "In a randomized experiment comparing two plant fertilizers on 30 identical tomato seedlings, why should all 30 seedlings receive the exact same amount of water and sunlight?","a": "To control extraneous variables so growth differences can be attributed to the fertilizer","lvl": 2,"choices": ["To control extraneous variables so growth differences can be attributed to the fertilizer","To double the sample size","To make the experiment double-blind","To reduce the number of treatments"],"steps": ["Ask why only the fertilizer should differ between groups.","Holding water and light steady prevents other growth causes.","Match that experimental control to the choices and check."]},{"id": "MM.22-06","std": "MM.22","q": "A student takes a simple random sample of 60 students from her school of 800 and finds 45% walk or bike to school (95% confidence interval: 32% to 58%). What is a valid conclusion?","a": "We are 95% confident that between 32% and 58% of all 800 students at the school walk or bike to school","lvl": 2,"choices": ["Exactly 45% of all 800 students walk or bike to school","Walking or biking causes better school attendance","Between 32% and 58% of all U.S. students walk or bike to school","We are 95% confident that between 32% and 58% of all 800 students at the school walk or bike to school"],"steps": ["Check what population the SRS came from and what a CI estimates.","Generalize only to the 800 students sampled, not the whole U.S.","Pick the valid confidence statement and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-07","std": "MM.22","q": "A student tests whether handwriting notes or typing notes leads to higher quiz scores by having an honors class handwrite and a standard class type. What is the design flaw and how should it be fixed?","a": "Class level is confounded with note-taking method; fix it by randomly assigning students within each class to handwrite or type","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Class level is confounded with note-taking method; fix it by randomly assigning students within each class to handwrite or type","Fix it by letting students choose whether to type or handwrite","Fix it by giving the honors class a harder quiz","There is no flaw because both classes take the same quiz"],"steps": ["Ask what differs between the two groups besides note-taking.","Think how to separate class level from the note-taking method.","Pick the critique and fix that match, then check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-08","std": "MM.22","q": "A student randomly assigns 30 volunteers from her PE class to do push-ups with upbeat music or in silence and finds the music group completes significantly more push-ups (p = 0.02). What is the proper scope of inference?","a": "Cause and effect can be concluded for these 30 volunteers, but results cannot be generalized to all teenagers because the subjects were not randomly sampled","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Results can be generalized to all teenagers, but causation cannot be concluded","Cause and effect can be concluded and generalized to all teenagers nationwide","Cause and effect can be concluded for these 30 volunteers, but results cannot be generalized to all teenagers because the subjects were not randomly sampled","Neither causation nor generalization is possible"],"steps": ["Separate random assignment (causation) from random sampling (scope).","Check which one this PE volunteer study used and which it lacked.","Pick the scope of inference matching both and check the choices."]},{"id": "MM.22-09","std": "MM.22","q": "Which design tests whether cold water or room-temperature water affects typing speed?","a": "Randomly assign 40 students to each water, same test, compare means","lvl": 3,"choices": ["Randomly assign 40 students to each water, same test, compare means","Let 40 students pick their water, then compare means","Give all 40 students cold water and compare to last year","Test one student with each water and compare"],"steps": ["Plan subjects, random assignment to two water groups, and timing.","Keep keyboard, passage, and room identical to control confounding.","Find the choice that matches your work, then rule out the others."]}]};
